-- title:
--   Lemma 4.2 (conjugation with an unused index)
-- statement:
--   Let $J \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ and $k \notin J$. For a column $c : J \to R$ and row $r : J \to R$, put $C_k(c) = \prod_{i\in J} x_{ik}(c_i)$ and $R_k(r) = \prod_{i\in J} x_{ki}(r_i)$ (the `columnElem`/`rowElem` of the `Definitions.BooneHigman_Steinberg` module -- well-defined regardless of product order, since generators in the same column, resp. row, commute).
--
--   If $w$ is a root word using only indices in $J$ (i.e. $w \in \mathrm{steinSub}(J)$, the subgroup generated by $\{x_{ab}(s) : a,b \in J\}$) and $A$ is $w$'s induced matrix on $J$ (i.e. $A = \pi(w)|_{J\times J}$, the restriction of $w$'s matrix image under $\pi : \mathrm{St}_n(R) \to \mathrm{GL}_n(R)$), then
--   $$w\,C_k(c)\,w^{-1} = C_k(Ac), \qquad w\,R_k(r)\,w^{-1} = R_k(rA^{-1}).$$
--
--   This is the preprint's Lemma 4.2, the "classical Steinberg centrality argument" recorded in its row-and-column form, which justifies the corner conjugations used throughout the rest of Section 4 (in particular the corollary that a root word omitting one index and having identity matrix is central in $\mathrm{St}_n(R)$, and Lemma 4.3's proof). The preprint fixes $n=6$; this formalization proves the statement for an arbitrary unital associative ring $R$ and arbitrary $n$, specializing to the preprint's case at $n=6$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Stated for a general root word $w \in \mathrm{steinSub}(J)$ (not just a single generator, as in the preprint's proof sketch) via induction on `Subgroup.closure_induction`; the single-generator case is itself a full verification of relations (13)/(reverse-(13)) at both the generator level and the matrix level. $Ac$ and $rA^{-1}$ are written out as the explicit matrix-vector sums $\sum_j A_{ij}c_j$ and $\sum_i r_i (A^{-1})_{ij}$, with $A^{-1} = \pi(w^{-1})$ (matrix inversion is not needed as a separate fact, since $\pi$ is a homomorphism). Zero `sorry` in the author's own proof, submitted separately.
-- source:
--   OpenAI, *Finite algebraic envelopes and the Boone-Higman conjecture*, OpenAI Math Release preprint, September 23, 2026, https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf, Lemma 4.2, p. 11.

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BooneHigman_Steinberg

namespace OAI
namespace FiniteAlgebraicEnvelopes

universe u

variable {n : ℕ} {R : Type u} [Ring R]

theorem lemma_4_2 (J : Finset (Fin n)) (k : Fin n) (hk : ∀ i ∈ J, i ≠ k)
    (w : SteinbergGroup n R) (hw : w ∈ steinSub J) (c r : Fin n → R) :
    w * columnElem J k hk c * w⁻¹ =
      columnElem J k hk
        (fun i => ∑ j ∈ J, (steinPi w : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) R) i j * c j) ∧
    w * rowElem J k hk r * w⁻¹ =
      rowElem J k hk
        (fun j => ∑ i ∈ J, r i * (steinPi w⁻¹ : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) R) i j) := by
  sorry

end FiniteAlgebraicEnvelopes
end OAI
